;;; An activator whose every argument is an int reads as no activator to
;;; *forbid* — M1e2 **W16**'s second shape, register row
;;; `W16/activator-all-int` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked and fixed
;;; by S1e2.2.16 (2026-11-04) from S1e2.2.7's second parked note.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `check` proposes `(checked 1)`, the one candidate. While `1` is not
;;; checked, `flag` asserts the activator `(watch 1)`; `watch` asserts the
;;; activator `(alarm 1)`; and `alarm` concludes `(false)`. Root decides
;;; nothing about `checked`, so its fixpoint has all three, and `(false)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `alarm`'s `(false)` rests on its activator, which `watch` derives from
;;; its own activator, which `flag` derives under `(absent (checked ?x))`,
;;; and `checked` is a relation a commitment can extend. That is *forbid*
;;; with F2's *per activator* (ruled 2026-11-04): the program is refused,
;;; as `activator-all-symbol.ein`, the same program with `(item a)`, is.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.16, R empty, every node dead, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` at
;;; `6a775f3d`, and under every mode, `ein oracle` and a debug build alike).
;;; *forbid* asked whether a plan has an activator as
;;; `!activator_args.is_empty()`, and `Plan::activator_args` keeps an
;;; activator's symbol arguments only, so root's plan of `alarm`, compiled
;;; from `(alarm 1)`, read as a rule with no activator and its cause was
;;; never looked for. `watch`'s plan was missed the same way, but `watch`'s
;;; activator relation is one a commitment can change, so *forbid* reads
;;; that rule from its pattern too and found what it derives; `alarm`'s is
;;; not, which is why the chain takes three rules (with two the pattern
;;; reading refuses an int activator as well). Since then the test reads
;;; whether the plan's rule has parameters, and every question about a
;;; commitment exits 1 naming `alarm`, as on the control; the line names no
;;; activator, because its label prints the symbols.
;;; `activator-all-nested.ein` is the same shape with a nested fact.

(relation item    T)
(relation checked T)
(relation watch   T)
(relation alarm   T)

(hrule check () :match (item ?x) :assert (checked ?x))

(rule flag () :match (and (item ?x) (absent (checked ?x))) :assert (watch ?x))
(rule watch (?n) :match (item ?n) :assert (alarm ?n))
(rule alarm (?k) :match (item ?k) :assert (false))

(item 1)
